  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>La Croix-Valmer stretches along 12 km of coastline, including 2 km of beaches, between Ramatuelle and Cavalaire-sur-Mer, according to the town hall. The area is a hilly peninsula of vineyards and pine forests, quieter than Saint-Tropez, just a few kilometres away. This page does not list a specific establishment: it brings together what you need to know about the dog rule before booking a campsite or rental in the area.</p>
      <h2>Good to know before booking</h2>
      <p>The La Croix-Valmer tourist office states, on the pages for its beaches (Débarquement, Gigaro, Héraclée, Sylvabelle, Briande), that dogs are banned from all beaches between 7am and 8pm, from 1 May to 30 October. Outside these hours and this period, they are allowed but must be kept on a leash. The town hall's beach rules also ban animals from the sand, except water rescue dogs and guide or assistance dogs. On the same office's pages relayed by Grimaud Tourisme, the wording is slightly different: dogs allowed on a leash from 10pm to 7am, banned from the swimming area.</p>
      <p>In practice, in high season, swimming with your dog is limited to late evening and early morning. Conditions in campsites and rentals (supplements, number of pets, size) vary from one establishment to another: calling directly before booking is the only way to get the exact rules.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: ask the town hall or the lifeguard station for the hours in force on the day of your stay, and bring a leash even outside the ban hours.</div>
      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The tourist office runs a free shuttle between the village and the beaches (Débarquement, Gigaro, Sylvabelle, Héraclée) from 1 June to 30 September, from about 8am to 8pm, and beach parking is paid from 1 June to 30 September, from 9am to 7pm. Briande beach, beyond Cap Lardier, can only be reached on foot, about 45 minutes from Gigaro, along the coastal path: bring water for your dog; the site is classified as fragile and lies within Port-Cros National Park.</p>
